35,2,1,talk,Rin,,"It takes way longer than I thought to get set up! Some people spend almost ten minutes just plugging things in, and since we all arrive at different times, the early birds get all the best spots!","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"The cost of re-running power is quite high per desk, and we are billed for floor area regardless of headcount. We are paying for empty space every month.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,"The noise levels are significantly higher near the printer and corridor. Also, the physio noted that getting a workstation ergonomically correct takes days of fine-tuning, so moving isn't just a quick swap.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Shion,,"It's interesting that Mizuki mentioned the same things. The noise gap between the corridor and the windows is quite stark, and the physio's note proves that comfort isn't an instant fix. It requires stability.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Yumi,,"I found out that daylight is a huge deal for everyone, but the current plan leaves some of us in the dark! Also, those unplanned shuffles last year sounded like such a headache for everyone involved.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Rin,,"@Mizuki, if it takes days to get a desk right, then those ten minutes of plugging in every morning are just the start of the problem! We can't keep switching things around.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Yumi, if unplanned shuffles were a headache, we should be cautious. Every time we change the layout, the facilities quote for power and network adds up quickly. It's an expensive process.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@Rin, exactly. Stability is key for physical health. If we move people frequently, we're essentially resetting their ergonomic progress, which isn't fair to those who have finally found a comfortable setup.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Shion,,"@Toshio, while costs are a factor, we shouldn't let the budget dictate our physical well-being. If we prioritize a layout that minimizes noise and maximizes stability, we avoid the need for those expensive, unplanned shuffles.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Yumi,,"@Shion, I totally agree! We should definitely prioritize our well-being. But @Toshio, if we plan it right now to make everyone happy, maybe we won't have to spend more money on shuffles later?","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Shion"",""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Rin,,"I agree! If we have a fixed spot, we don't waste those precious minutes every morning. I don't want to spend my day just fighting with cables and screens!","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"Planning for happiness is a goal, but we must be realistic. If we over-engineer a layout that is too rigid, any future team growth will force another expensive re-run of power and network.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,"@Toshio, realism also means acknowledging that some spots are objectively worse than others. If we just focus on the budget, we're ignoring the fact that the noise and ergonomics create an uneven playing field for the team.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Shion,,"@Mizuki, you've hit the nail on the head. A ""realistic"" plan that ignores the physical toll of a noisy or poorly fitted desk is just a plan for future inefficiency. We need a layout that respects the individual's space.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Yumi,,"I feel for everyone who's been struggling with noise or bad lighting! If we can just find a way to balance the costs with a layout that makes us all feel welcome and comfortable, it would be amazing!","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Rin,,"I'm with Yumi! Let's make a plan that's cozy and easy. If we just pick a layout that works for everyone and stick to it, we save time and money!","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":true,""norm"":[{""code"":""trailing_over"",""token"":""Over"",""tail"":""Over""}]}"
51,2,4,pass,Rin,,Over,"{""forced"":false,""empty"":false,""trailing"":true}"
52,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"I agree that stability is beneficial, but we must remember that we are paying for floor area regardless of how we use it. A layout that is too sprawling will be a waste of our limited budget.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Mizuki,,"@Toshio, efficiency isn't just about floor area; it's about the people using it. If we ignore the disparity in noise and comfort, we're just optimizing for space while sacrificing the team's focus and health.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Shion,,"It seems we are circling the same point. Toshio is optimizing for the floor, while the rest of us are optimizing for the people. A layout that is ""efficient"" on paper but physically draining is a failure.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Yumi,,"I think we can find a middle ground! If we create a layout that's both space-efficient and thoughtful about our needs, we can keep the budget happy and the team smiling! Let's be kind to ourselves!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
