61,3,1,talk,Rin,,"Option A sounds like a nightmare! We'd waste so much time every morning plugging in cables, and the early birds would always get the best spots. I definitely vote against that!","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"Option A is inefficient and costly in the long run. If we are paying for floor area regardless of headcount, we should utilize the space with a structure that avoids daily chaos and constant reconfiguration.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Mizuki,,Option A is completely illogical given the ergonomic needs we discussed. Forcing people to reset their workstation every single day is a recipe for physical strain and inefficiency. It's simply not a viable choice.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Shion,,Option A is a disaster. It ignores the physical toll of resetting a workstation and the noise disparities we've identified. It's the opposite of the stability and comfort we need to actually function.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,1,talk,Yumi,,"I agree with everyone! Option A sounds so stressful, and I'd hate for anyone to feel left out or uncomfortable just because they arrived a few minutes later. Let's pick something more stable!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Rin,,"What about Option B? Rotating sounds like an adventure, but wouldn't we just be doing that annoying plugging-in and adjusting thing all over again every time we shift? That sounds like a waste of time!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"Option B is financially reckless. Every rotation would likely require re-running power or network cables to suit the new block, and we've already seen how expensive those facilities quotes are. It's a recurring cost we can't afford.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@Toshio, you're right about the cost, but more importantly, Option B just delays the inevitable. It doesn't solve the disparity in seat quality; it just forces everyone to suffer through the worst spots on a schedule.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Shion,,Exactly. Option B is just a scheduled redistribution of discomfort. It doesn't eliminate the noise or the ergonomic struggle; it simply ensures that everyone is periodically miserable. It's a superficial solution to a structural problem.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,2,talk,Yumi,,"@Shion, that's a bit sad to think about! I don't want anyone to be miserable, even on a schedule. Plus, those unplanned shuffles were already such a headache; why would we want to do it on purpose?","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Rin,,"Option C is the best! We get our own special spot, no more fighting with cables every morning, and we can finally just start our work right away. It's the most relaxing way!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Toshio,,Option C is the most rational choice. It provides the stability the team needs while minimizing the recurring costs of re-running power and network. It is the only option that respects both our budget and our time.,"{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Mizuki,,"Option C is the most logical for ergonomics and budget, but we must be honest: some desks are objectively worse. If we go with C, we need a fair, transparent way to decide who takes the noisy spots.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Shion,,"@Mizuki, I agree. Option C is the only one that allows for the stability required for a proper ergonomic setup. However, the ""fairness"" of the assignment is where the real decision lies. We cannot ignore the noise gap.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,3,talk,Yumi,,"I agree! If we go with Option C, we can just talk it out and make sure everyone is happy with their spot. It'll be like picking seats for a fun trip together! I vote for C!","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Rin,,I'm voting for C! It's the only way to save our mornings and keep things simple. Let's just pick our favorite spots and be done with it! I vote for C!,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Toshio,,I will also vote for C. It is the only option that ensures financial stability and operational efficiency while providing the consistency the team requires. I vote for C.,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Mizuki,,"I'll vote for C as well. It's the only practical choice for our health and budget, provided we explicitly agree on how to handle the less desirable seats so no one is unfairly burdened. I vote for C.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Shion,,"I'll vote for C. It is the only option that guarantees the physical stability and acoustic environment necessary for long-term focus. As long as we acknowledge the quality of each spot, it's the only logical choice. I vote for C.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
80,3,4,talk,Yumi,,I'm so happy we all agree! Let's make our office a place where everyone feels comfortable and welcome. I'm voting for C too! I vote for C!,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
